# Icons & Animation

## Icon System (current state)

All 46 icons in `assets/` are **inline SVGs with `stroke="currentColor"`**. There are no `<img>` tags with base64 data URIs — that approach was fully retired. Every icon automatically inherits its color from CSS, enabling theming, hover/disabled states, and path-level animation with no extra work.

## Icon Sources

| Use case | Source |
|----------|--------|
| Standard icons | `lucide-static` (installed) or copy from [lucide.dev](https://lucide.dev) |
| Custom / bespoke shapes | Figma → export SVG, then apply prep steps below |
| Animated icons | Figma (authored) or CSS `@keyframes` on the inline SVG element |

## Adding an Icon

1. **Find the Lucide equivalent** — most UI icons map directly.
2. **Copy to `assets/`** with the `ICO-` prefix:
   ```bash
   cp node_modules/lucide-static/icons/your-icon.svg assets/ICO-your-icon.svg
   ```
3. **Strip `width` and `height`** from the root `<svg>` (keep `viewBox`). Lucide icons already have `stroke="currentColor"` — no other edits needed.
4. **Reference in `ui.html`** as a plain `<img>` tag:
   ```html
   <img src="./assets/ICO-your-icon.svg" alt="" class="your-classes">
   ```
   The build pipeline replaces every `<img src="*.svg">` with the raw `<svg>` markup, preserving `class` attributes.

## Making a Custom (Figma) SVG Inline-Ready

Four things to fix on every Figma export:

```
① Remove width/height from <svg> root        (keep viewBox only)
② Remove <g clip-path="url(#...)"> wrapper   (promote paths to root)
③ stroke="white" → stroke="currentColor"
④ Remove entire <defs><clipPath>...</defs>   block
```

**Shortcut:** uncheck "Clip content" on the frame before exporting — eliminates ② and ④ at source. Then run SVGO:
```bash
npx svgo assets/ICO-new-icon.svg
```

## Dynamic Icon Swapping (JS)

When an icon changes at runtime (e.g. eye open↔closed, lock, sizing mode, nav mode), do **not** use an `<img>` tag. Instead:

- Store the SVG as a **single-line** markup string constant in `ui.ts` (no literal newlines — they break JS string literals after build inlining):
  ```typescript
  const ICON_EYE_OPEN = '<svg xmlns="..." viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" ...><path .../></svg>';
  ```
- Put the `id` on the **parent `<span class="nav-icon">`**, not on an inner element.
- Swap with `element.innerHTML = ICON_CONSTANT`.

## Build Pipeline

`esbuild.config.js` post-processes `dist/ui.html` after JS inlining:

- `<img src="*.svg">` anywhere in the HTML → replaced with the raw `<svg>` markup
- SVG content is **collapsed to a single line** before injection (strips XML prolog, HTML comments/license headers, all newlines) — this is required to prevent SyntaxErrors when the tag appears inside a JS string literal in the bundled script
- `class` attribute from `<img>` is merged into the `<svg>` root

⚠️ Never put multi-line SVG markup inside a JS string constant in `ui.ts`. The build inlines the entire JS bundle into a `<script>` tag; literal newlines inside string literals cause a parse-time `SyntaxError` that silently kills the entire plugin UI.

## CSS Targeting

Selectors use `svg`, not `img`:
```css
.nav-icon svg   { width: var(--icon-size-md); height: var(--icon-size-md); }
.icon svg       { color: var(--theme-icon-primary); }
.nav-button:disabled .nav-icon svg { opacity: 0.4; }
```

## Animation Tiers

**Tier 1 — CSS `@keyframes`** (zero runtime cost, works on any inline SVG element):
```css
#nav-up-btn:active .nav-icon    { animation: nudge-up    0.28s ease-out; }
#refresh-anchors:active .icon   { animation: spin-once   0.5s ease-in-out; }
#nav-delete:active .nav-icon    { animation: shake       0.45s ease-out; }
```

**Tier 2 — Animated SVG** (self-contained `<style>` block inside the `.svg` file):
- `stroke-dasharray` / `stroke-dashoffset` for draw-on effects
- Prototype by opening the `.svg` directly in a browser — no build needed
- Only reach for this when path-level animation is required

**Tier 3 — Lottie** (dormant — infrastructure exists, not wired up):
- `lottie-web` is installed; `src/ui/shared/lottie.ts` has init/play/pause/destroy helpers
- Build system (`esbuild.config.js`) can inline Lottie JSON via `data-lottie-src` attributes
- Sample asset: `assets/sample-loading.json`; docs: `docs/LOTTIE_SUPPORT.md`
- Currently **not imported or called** from any active UI code — no `data-lottie-src` in HTML
- To enable: import from `lottie.ts`, uncomment `lottie.loadAnimation()`, add `data-lottie-src` to HTML
- Lottie JSON is inlined at build time, so it works within Figma's `networkAccess: none` constraint

No Rive — requires a runtime that fetches external resources, incompatible with Figma's sandbox.

## Prototype / Iteration Workflow

```bash
npm run sync:prototype            # full build + patch prototype/plugin.html
npm run sync:prototype --no-build # patch only (shim.js edits, no rebuild needed)
npx serve prototype               # http://localhost:3000/plugin.html
```

Animation playground: `prototype/anim-playground.html` — iterate there first, then move approved `@keyframes` into `styles.css`.
